cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or reference this paper, please cite it using the preferred citation below."
title: "Prime cadences: Ihara zeta, graphene, and voice-leading homology on the Tonnetz"
authors:
  - name: "Ergentics"
version: "0.3.1"
date-released: "2026-11-25"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
keywords:
  - "neo-Riemannian theory"
  - "Tonnetz"
  - "Ihara zeta function"
  - "Ramanujan graphs"
  - "graphene"
  - "Mumford curves"
  - "Schottky groups"
  - "Cerednik-Drinfeld uniformisation"
  - "curves over finite fields"
  - "voice-leading homology"
abstract: >-
  The 24 major and minor triads, joined by the neo-Riemannian moves P, L, R,
  form a 3-regular graph X. We call the primes of its Ihara zeta function prime
  cadences and prove a number of exact statements about them. X is the
  honeycomb lattice on a 12-cell torus; its zeta zeros are the tight-binding
  band energies, equal to the moduli of the triad's discrete Fourier
  coefficients, and X is Ramanujan. Over Q, zeta_X^{-1} factors into Weil
  polynomials over F_2. The sequence of moving notes alone determines a cadence
  (tune theorem), and in every equal-tempered system with any step set the tune
  determines the cadence, apart from one explicit family. The voices' net
  winding is a torus homology coordinate, asymptotically Gaussian with variance
  m/24; its fourth cumulant is isotropic and hexagonal anisotropy first appears
  at order six. Twisting by the winding preserves the Riemann hypothesis on an
  explicit window. Three involutions of PGL_2(Q_2), one per move, generate a
  Schottky group whose Mumford curve C has genus 13, reduction graph X and
  automorphism group the transposition/inversion group. We compute its
  component group and gonality, find its order-12 factor in an explicit genus-5
  curve over F_2 (genus 5 is minimal), and show via a trace invariant and
  2-adic edge-length obstructions that C admits no Cerednik-Drinfeld
  uniformisation except possibly by a group with torsion at a ramified place
  (e_v=2, f_v=1 forces Eichler mass >= 36) or an inert place (where
  inversion-free uniformisation at even f_v >= 2 forces Q(zeta_{2^{f_v}+1})^+
  subset E, sharp via an explicit non-arithmetic group Gamma_5 subset
  PGL_2(Q_4) with edge inversions). No Shimura curve with torsion-free
  uniformisation has reduction graph X over a totally real field of degree at
  most 5, or of degree at least 9 (sharpened unconditionally from n <= 14 to
  n <= 8 via a three-pincer Hilbert class field obstruction, which also
  eliminates sextic narrow-class ratios u in {16, 32}).
